# at-spi2-core -- the accessibility bus and registry, AND the ATK library.
#
# THE ATK POINT IS THE REASON THIS IS IN THE FIRST WAVE. mutter declares an
# `atk` dependency (mutter-48.4/meson.build), and there is no atk package to
# satisfy it: ATK and at-spi2-atk were absorbed into at-spi2-core and neither
# ships as a separate tarball in BLFS 12.4 any more. This one package provides
# atk.pc, atspi-2.pc and the bus launcher that used to be three.
#
# It also has no cross-chain dependencies at all -- dbus, glib and libxml2 are
# already in the tree -- which is why it moved up from wave 2: gtk 3.24.50 needs
# it, and gtk3 is what libnma needs, and libnma is what gnome-control-center
# needs. Landing it early unblocks that chain a whole wave sooner.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/at-spi2-core/2.56/at-spi2-core-2.56.4.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=dbe35b951499e1d6f1fb552c2e0a09cea7cba2adf6c2eba0b2c85b6c094a3a02
SRC_DIR=at-spi2-core-2.56.4

# -Dgtk2_atk_adaptor=false IS LOAD-BEARING. It defaults to TRUE upstream and
# builds the ATK bridge for GTK 2 -- a toolkit this tree does not package and
# never will. Left at its default it is a hard build failure at best and an
# unnoticed dependency at worst.
#
# -Dx11=disabled keeps the Wayland-first rule: the option is an 'auto' feature,
# so it would silently switch itself on wherever the X client libraries happen
# to be installed -- and they ARE installed here, because several GNOME
# components link them regardless of backend. An auto feature in a tree that
# carries the optional dependency is not optional in practice; it has to be
# pinned to be predictable.
#
# -Duse_systemd=false: defaults to true, and there is no systemd here. elogind
# provides a libsystemd.pc symlink, so this would otherwise configure against it
# and generate user units that nothing will ever start.
MESON_ARGS="-Dgtk2_atk_adaptor=false -Dx11=disabled -Duse_systemd=false -Ddocs=false -Dintrospection=enabled"
